(N1) Lindelof ORDER of the two competing objects
     order = limsup_{R->inf} log log M(R) / log R,  M(R)=max_{|t|=R}|F(t)|
     The log log M / log R limit converges like 1/log R and is useless at
     attainable R.  We instead test the two DEFINING growth laws directly:
        Psi_g :  log M(R)/R   -> max_[0,1]|g| = 7   (order 1, finite type)
        D     :  log M(R)/R   -> infinity, log M(R)/R^2 -> 1  (order 2)
     Order 1 with finite type is exactly what (E2)+(E3) force; order 2 is
     incompatible with being an E-function.

      R      [Psi] logM/R      [D] logM/R       [D] logM/R^2
       10     6.60961437002      9.7009331826     0.97009331826
       20     6.76994353134     19.8156187238    0.990780936191
       40     6.86759231834     39.8904571527    0.997261428819
       80     6.92511922457     79.9365613041    0.999207016301

     Sharp model: the max of |g| on [0,1] sits at the ENDPOINT u=0 with
     g(0)=7, g'(0)=-5, so one-sided Laplace gives Psi_g(R) ~ e^{7R}/(5R):
        R      log M(R) - 7R + log(5R)   (-> 0)
       10          0.008166705588
       20          0.004040812801
       40          0.002010100105
       80          0.001002512422
