(N2) the NONVANISHING form is FALSE over C (so algebraicity is the whole content)
  [PASS] int_0^1 e^{2 pi i t} dt = 0 exactly   |I| = 6.16544e-106
     degree-2 zeros: solve int_0^1 e^{a t^2 + b t} dt = 0 for b, at fixed a.
     a=         1.0  b=(-1.0 + 5.980447289018471885576708767695294652354j)   |I|=6.089e-106
     a=        -1.0  b=(1.0 + 6.618231563474704611910496122627523533937j)   |I|=6.139e-106
     a=         2.0  b=(-2.0 + 5.706820294245798800205103671620160831093j)   |I|=5.915e-106
     a=(0.0 + 1.0j)  b=(0.3177318391719765157304018277076280660912 + 5.267087563631907648994037828093069718886j)   |I|=7.31e-106
  [PASS] degree-2 vanishing locus is NONEMPTY over C   4 zeros located to 70+ digits

     STRUCTURE (exact).  For REAL a the located zeros all have Re(b) = -a.
     Reason: put b = -a + i*beta and v = t - 1/2.  Then
        int_0^1 e^{a t^2 + b t} dt = e^{-a/4 + i beta/2} * int_{-1/2}^{1/2} e^{a v^2} (cos(beta v) + i sin(beta v)) dv,
     the sine part is odd and drops, so the integral vanishes exactly when
        K_a(beta) := int_{-1/2}^{1/2} e^{a v^2} cos(beta v) dv = 0,
     a REAL equation.  At a = 0, K_0(beta) = 2 sin(beta/2)/beta, first zero
     beta = 2 pi -- Lindemann's case, recovered as the a -> 0 limit.
  [PASS]   Re(b) = -a exactly for a=1.0   Re(b)+a = 0.0
  [PASS]   Re(b) = -a exactly for a=-1.0   Re(b)+a = 0.0
  [PASS]   Re(b) = -a exactly for a=2.0   Re(b)+a = 0.0
  [PASS]   K_0 first zero = 2 pi to 80 digits   |beta - 2pi| = 5.738e-102
     beta(a=1) = 5.98044728901847188557670876769529465235376872344257938568394

