(N3) THM-3018 sec 4b Laplace growth for Psi_g -- the step that forbids
     Psi_g from being a POLYNOMIAL (needed to convert Beukers into transcendence)
     A = max|g| = 4.0 at u* = 1.0,  theta = -1.57079632679
     u* = 1 is an ENDPOINT of [0,1], so the Laplace exponent is s^{-1}
     (one-sided), not the interior-critical-point s^{-1/2}.
       s      |Psi(s e^{i th})| * s * e^{-sA}    (-> 1/|g'(u*)|)
        20    0.19721495952151
        40    0.19666663257542
        80    0.19639158482714
       160    0.19625390196422
       320    0.19618502804636
     predicted limit 1/|g'(u*)| = 0.19611613513818
  [PASS] |Psi(s e^{i th})| ~ e^{sA}/(|g'(u*)| s): normalised ratio stabilises   last ratio-of-ratios = 0.99964906
  [PASS] and it converges to the predicted 1/|g'(u*)|   measured*|g'(u*)| = 1.000351286
     => Psi_g grows EXPONENTIALLY on that ray, so it is not a polynomial,
        and a fortiori not identically 1.  (This alone re-proves FC(2).)

